const SECTION_1_SCRIPT = `International English Language Testing System. Listening Practice Simulation.
Section 1. You will hear an engineering consultation between a municipal transport planner and a technical contractor inspecting an autonomous electric vehicle charging depot.
First, you have some time to look at questions 1 to 5.
Now listen carefully and answer questions 1 to 5.

Planner: "Good morning, North Midlands Municipal Transport and Fleet Logistics Depot. Senior Infrastructure Engineer Davies speaking. How can I help you today?"
Callum: "Good morning, Engineer Davies. This is Callum from Volt-Grid Infrastructure Solutions. I'm calling to finalize the technical installation inspection logs and safety compliance records for our municipality's rapid charging hub."
Planner: "Splendid! We have been awaiting your site sign-off before our joint municipal audit review next week. First, for our civil procurement audit and compliance register, may I confirm the lead electrical contractor surname, please?"
Callum: "Yes, certainly. My surname is Trevithick. That is spelled T-R-E-V-I-T-H-I-C-K. And my given name is Callum."
Planner: "Thank you, Mr. Trevithick: that is T-R-E-V-I-T-H-I-C-K. And what is your direct radio link or mobile contact where the substation control room can reach you on site during commissioning?"
Callum: "You can reach me at 07822 550198... Ah, apologies, Engineer Davies! The last three digits were updated following our recent substation relocation. My active direct site number is 07822 550443."
Planner: "Right, let me make that amendment immediately in our municipal dispatch system: 07822 550443. Perfect. Now, regarding the energy storage trial across the heavy charging bays: did your team deploy the Lithium Iron Phosphate cells or the Sodium Solid-State cells?"
Callum: "We initially tested the Lithium Iron Phosphate packs in our preliminary pilot, but after rigorous thermodynamic modeling in sub-zero and peak heat conditions, we finalized the Sodium Solid-State cells due to their exceptional high temperature stability and non-flammable properties."
Planner: "Superb! Sodium Solid-State cells are indeed the safest option for municipal infrastructure located in dense urban zones. We want to avoid any chemical thermal runaway risks near residential zones. And how many municipal transit minibuses can undergo simultaneous rapid overnight charging?"
Callum: "The heavy-duty bus depot has been calibrated to charge exactly 12 vehicles per bay."
Planner: "Understood: exactly 12 vehicles per bay across the covered parking apron. That gives our municipal fleet plenty of overnight turnaround headroom. And lastly, for our statutory fire and emergency safety inspection: where exactly is the master Emergency Power Cutoff switch located?"
Callum: "When inspectors enter through the heavy blast doors into the main electrical conduit room, it is not on the north console where the old switches were. Instead, it is housed inside the red safety vault installed immediately beside the ventilation intake."
Planner: "Inside the red safety vault beside the ventilation intake. Splendid! That meets all statutory guidelines and fire safety codes. Thank you very much, Mr. Trevithick."

Now look at questions 6 to 10.
Listen carefully and answer questions 6 to 10.

Planner: "Now, regarding testing schedule deadlines: when will the full high-voltage transformer commissioning trial take place?"
Callum: "The national grid operator confirmed the high-voltage energization test for Tuesday, the 24th of October."
Planner: "Tuesday, 24th October recorded on the calendar. And what is the peak charging capacity per transformer unit?"
Callum: "Each unit delivers 350 kilowatts of rapid continuous direct current."
Planner: "350 kilowatts DC. That will easily recharge our municipal transit fleet during short turnaround windows between morning and evening commuter services. And regarding facility maintenance access: where should technician vans park during weekly servicing?"
Callum: "Technician vehicles must park in Bay 3 directly adjacent to the control tower."
Planner: "Bay 3 adjacent to control tower. Excellent! That keeps the main perimeter roadway clear for autonomous sweeping machines and snowplows in winter. And what is the emergency hotline number for the substation engineer on duty?"
Callum: "The direct hotline is 0800 449 1102."
Planner: "0800 449 1102. Perfect. And what is the statutory servicing frequency for technician vans to conduct preventive maintenance checks?"
Callum: "Our engineering contract stipulates weekly inspections of coolant levels, contact points, and high-voltage cable insulation."
Planner: "Weekly servicing access confirmed. In addition, our civil electrical engineering inspectors will accompany your technician team during the initial four weeks to verify that harmonic distortions on the municipal power feed remain well within statutory grid tolerance margins. We have also installed optical infrared thermal cameras above each bus bay to monitor charging cable temperatures in real time, transmitting alerts directly to your operations console if heat anomalies are detected. The municipal transport authority considers this autonomous bus depot the centerpiece of our regional green transit corridor. Our engineering facilities supervisor will ensure Bay 3 is kept clear for your vans every Monday morning, and security badges will be pre-authorized at the main gate. Furthermore, our environmental health inspector will attend to verify zero fluid leaks from the transformer oil reservoirs. We have also installed automated seismic vibration sensors around the transformer slab to detect earth tremors. The depot management appreciates your thorough cooperation in ensuring that our regional electrification milestone is delivered on schedule without compromising grid stability."
Callum: "You are most welcome, Engineer Davies. Our technicians take tremendous pride in building clean, durable public infrastructure. We will see your inspection team on site on the 24th. Goodbye!"

That is the end of Section 1. You now have half a minute to check your answers.`;

const SECTION_2_SCRIPT = `Section 2. You will hear an orientation talk given by Facility Director Sarah Jenkins to new members visiting the Highfield Community Sports and Aquatic Centre.
First, you have some time to look at questions 11 to 15.
Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 15.

Sarah: "Good morning, everyone, and welcome to the newly expanded Highfield Community Sports and Aquatic Centre! My name is Sarah Jenkins, and I am the Facility Director. Following a £4 million architectural modernization program funded jointly by the municipal council and National Lottery Sport, our complex now boasts state-of-the-art athletic, hydrotherapy, and strength-conditioning facilities. Over the past eighteen months, our civil and acoustic engineers have completely reconfigured this six-acre recreational hub to serve residents of all ages and physical abilities, from toddler swimming groups to regional competitive sprinters.

Let me orient you using the center layout map displayed in the main reception lobby and printed on page two of your welcome brochure. We are currently standing at Point A, the Central Atrium and Reception Desk, right past the automated optical security turnstiles, customer service kiosk, and pro-forma sports equipment retail shop.

Immediately to your left through the soundproof glass double doors, at Point B, you will find our Olympic-sized 50-meter Competition Pool, equipped with FINA-certified automated touch-timing pads, underwater biomechanical stroke analysis cameras, and hydraulic moveable floor depth bulkheads that allow our aquatics team to adjust water depth for junior learners or regional water polo tournaments. The water filtration system uses advanced ultraviolet and ozone sterilization, eliminating heavy chemical chlorine odors and eye irritation.

If you head down the central tiled hallway past the organic juice bar to Point C, you will enter the Hydrotherapy & Thermal Spa Suite, featuring heated mineral salt-water pools, cedarwood Finnish sauna rooms, eucalyptus steam cabins, and contrast ice plunge basins designed specifically for muscular recovery and post-injury physical rehabilitation.

Directly opposite the thermal spa at Point D is the Indoor Climbing Wall, featuring twenty-meter vertical lead-climbing routes, sixteen automated magnetic belay devices, and dedicated cushioned bouldering caves suitable for novices and competitive mountaineers alike. Qualified mountaineering instructors supervise the climbing zone continuously throughout opening hours.

Finally, at the rear of the main building overlooking the outdoor Tartan athletics track, situated at Point F, lies our Strength & Conditioning Gymnasium, housing fifty regenerative cardio stations, biometric rowing ergometers, and Olympic weightlifting platforms with panoramic floor-to-ceiling views across the municipal parklands."

Now look at questions 16 to 20.
Listen carefully and answer questions 16 to 20.

Sarah: "Now, let me share essential administrative information regarding membership tiers, seasonal opening hours, aquatic safety rules, and personal coaching sessions so that you can make full use of our amenities from day one.

Our standard monthly adult pass costs £42, which includes unlimited access to the strength gymnasium, 50-meter swimming pool, thermal spa suite, and scheduled group fitness classes. Full-time university students and senior citizens qualify for our discounted concession rate of £28 per month upon presenting valid photographic identification at the membership desk.

The complex opens daily from 06:00 AM on weekdays, closing at 22:00 PM, allowing early-bird commuters to complete their swim or gym workout before heading to the office. Weekend operating hours run from 07:30 AM to 20:00 PM on both Saturdays and Sundays.

For statutory safety reasons, children under twelve must be accompanied by a responsible adult in all aquatic zones at all times, and non-swimmers must remain within designated shallow roped areas under direct parental supervision. Royal Life Saving Society qualified lifeguards patrol the poolside elevated chairs continuously during all public sessions.

Finally, complimentary changing room locker storage requires a refundable £1 coin or returnable brass token. Alternatively, waterproof keyless RFID silicone wristbands can be purchased permanently at reception for £3, allowing members to lock digital lockers and scan through entry turnstiles seamlessly without carrying loose change or metal keys. We also host junior competitive swim training squads on Tuesday evenings and low-impact senior water aerobics on Thursday mornings. In addition, our on-site sports physiotherapy clinic on the first floor offers biomechanical gait assessments and sports massage appointments bookable online. Our nutrition smoothie lounge adjoining the atrium provides cold-pressed juices and post-workout protein shakes from 08:00 AM until closing. All members are encouraged to download our companion smartphone app to track workout metrics and book court reservations up to fourteen days in advance. Thank you for your attention, and welcome to Highfield Community Sports Centre!"

That is the end of Section 2. You now have half a minute to check your answers.`;

const SECTION_3_SCRIPT = `Section 3. You will hear a tutorial discussion between Dr. Robert Langdon, an urban hydrology tutor, and two postgraduate engineering students, Chloe and Ethan, evaluating sustainable urban drainage systems (SuDS).
First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 25.
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 25.

Dr. Langdon: "Good morning, Chloe, Ethan. Let us review your empirical field report on sustainable urban drainage systems—or SuDS—installed across the Manchester East commercial and residential redevelopment project. Chloe, to open our evaluation, what is the core hydrogeological objective of permeable interlocking pavements compared to traditional impervious asphalt road surfaces?"

Chloe: "Dr. Langdon, traditional hot-rolled asphalt creates an impermeable waterproof seal across urban catchments that forces nearly 100% of surface rainwater directly into subterranean piped storm sewers, rapidly overwhelming municipal capacity and precipitating flash flooding downstream. In contrast, permeable interlocking concrete pavers feature aggregate-filled joints that allow storm runoff to infiltrate directly through graded crushed-granite gravel sub-base layers into underlying soil aquifers, filtering out suspended particulates, brake-pad heavy metals, and petroleum hydrocarbons naturally via microbial biofilms."

Ethan: "Furthermore, our automated tipping-bucket rain gauge and piezometer field infiltration measurements proved that permeable pavers reduced peak stormwater runoff discharge volumes by over fifty-five percent during high-intensity autumn cloudburst events, taking immense hydraulic pressure off aged Victorian municipal storm conduits. The subterranean crushed-gravel sub-base layers essentially function as distributed underground detention reservoirs, attenuating flood hydrographs and slowly discharging purified groundwater over twelve to twenty-four hours rather than fifteen minutes."

Dr. Langdon: "Impressive empirical field data. And Ethan, what did your instrumentation reveal regarding vegetated retention swales and extensive sedum green roofs across the high-density residential blocks?"

Ethan: "Extensive green roofs proved highly multi-functional: they mitigate the summer urban heat island effect via evapotranspiration while absorbing the first fifteen millimeters of incident rainfall directly within their lightweight expanded-shale soil substrate, delaying storm runoff hydrograph peaks by up to two hours compared to conventional slate or bitumen roofs."

Chloe: "Though our longitudinal maintenance audit highlighted one critical operational vulnerability: vegetated roadside retention swales require scheduled mechanical silt and leaf-mulch removal every three years to prevent fine sediment crusting and clogging from compromising soil infiltration capacity. In high-traffic avenues lined with deciduous trees, bi-annual vacuum sweeping of porous paver joints and inspection of overflow inlet grates are also essential to ensure stormwater flows smoothly into the vegetated detention basins without backing up onto adjacent cycle pathways."

Now look at questions 26 to 30.
Listen carefully and answer questions 26 to 30.

Dr. Langdon: "A very perceptive engineering observation, Chloe. Long-term maintenance lifecycle planning is precisely where municipal civil drainage schemes frequently falter after hand-over. Now let us confirm your final manuscript task allocations before next week's symposium deadline: writing the field infiltration methodology, drawing the CAD swale cross-sections, authoring the municipal cost-benefit analysis, compiling bibliographical references, and drafting the executive summary."

Ethan: "Since I calibrated the piezometers and compiled the infiltration field data logs and hydrograph flow-rate charts, I will draft the infiltration methodology section."

Chloe: "And I will create the scaled AutoCAD engineering cross-sections for the vegetated retention swales, subterranean gravel detention basins, and permeable pavement sub-base layers."

Ethan: "Regarding the economic evaluation: since Chloe performed the comparative literature review on municipal flood damage mitigation vs. conventional sewer expansion costs, she will author the municipal cost-benefit analysis."

Chloe: "Which leaves Ethan with compiling the bibliographical references and verifying that all hydrological citations match institutional civil engineering guidelines."

Dr. Langdon: "And as standard practice for joint departmental monographs, you will co-author the executive summary jointly to articulate your engineering conclusions concisely. Outstanding distribution of responsibilities! Please make sure your completed digital dossier is uploaded to our civil engineering portal by 17:00 PM on Thursday, and ensure all hydrological attenuation graphs include standard deviation error bars representing seasonal sampling variance. We will review the final proof together in our Friday morning seminar before submitting the technical dossier to the regional municipal water authority."

Chloe & Ethan: "We will make sure it is uploaded on schedule, Dr. Langdon! Thank you for the guidance."

That is the end of Section 3. You now have half a minute to check your answers.`;

const SECTION_4_SCRIPT = `Section 4. You will hear an agricultural biotechnology lecture given by Professor Arthur Pendelton on vertical urban farming and hydroponic food production.
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

Professor Pendelton: "Good morning, ladies and gentlemen. In today's agricultural biotechnology lecture, we examine Vertical Urban Farming—the Controlled Environment Agriculture practice of cultivating horticultural crops in vertically stacked layers within climate-controlled indoor architectural structures.

As global demographic projections indicate the human population will approach ten billion by the year 2050, with over seventy percent residing in dense metropolitan regions, traditional horizontal open-field agriculture faces acute ecological, land, and hydrological constraints. Conventional soil-based agriculture currently consumes roughly seventy percent of global freshwater withdrawals and accounts for widespread topsoil erosion, chemical pesticide drift, nitrogen-phosphorus fertilizer runoff, and tropical deforestation. Vertical farming directly addresses these interlocking crises by decoupling food production from arable land, replacing field soil with nutrient-rich recirculating aqueous mineral solutions—termed Hydroponics—or high-pressure atomized nutrient mist sprays—termed Aeroponics.

In closed-loop aeroponic cultivation chambers, plant roots suspended in sterile air are misted periodically by ultrasonic nozzles with precise ionic nutrient solutions, reducing crop water consumption by up to ninety-five percent compared to conventional open-field irrigation. Because transpired humidity and excess nutrient solutions are condensed, captured, sterilized via ultraviolet filtration, and continuously recycled within a sealed loop, agricultural fertilizer runoff into natural rivers and coastal estuaries is entirely eliminated, preserving fragile aquatic ecosystems from toxic algal blooms and eutrophication.

Furthermore, solid-state light-emitting diode—or LED—lighting technology allows plant physiologists to optimize photosynthetic photon flux spectra—specifically tuning narrow-band blue (450 nanometer) and red (660 nanometer) wavelength ratios—to maximize chlorophyll absorption and accelerate photosynthesis without generating wasteful thermal heat output. Under tailored photoperiods and enriched carbon dioxide atmospheres, crops grow up to two hundred percent faster year-round regardless of external seasonal weather, drought, or geographic latitude. Nutrient-dense leafy greens, culinary herbs, microgreens, and dwarf strawberries can be harvested continuously inside repurposed urban warehouses, shipping containers, or underground tunnels within municipal boundaries, slashing cold-chain food transportation miles and post-harvest spoilage emissions. In specialized multi-story vertical facilities operating in Tokyo, Singapore, and Rotterdam, stacked cultivation racks yield up to thirty times more edible biomass per square meter of land footprint than conventional horizontal farmland.

However, high initial capital expenditure and continuous electrical energy consumption for LED arrays and HVAC dehumidification controls remain significant commercial hurdles, particularly for high-calorie staple cereal crops like wheat, rice, or maize. To overcome these thermodynamic costs, agricultural engineers are integrating building-mounted photovoltaic solar arrays, anaerobic digestion of plant root waste, and industrial waste-heat recovery systems from adjacent data centers or district heating networks to lower operational costs and improve commercial viability. When combined with localized geothermal heat pumps, modern vertical farms can achieve net-neutral carbon operational profiles even in sub-arctic northern climates.

Furthermore, horticultural researchers are pioneering biological pest control within closed indoor biomes, deploying beneficial predatory insects such as ladybugs and parasitoid wasps to eradicate stray aphids without a single drop of synthetic chemical pesticide. Automation also extends to robotic computer-vision harvesting arms equipped with multispectral deep-learning classifiers that identify optimal leaf ripeness and harvest delicate crops without bruising tender plant tissues. In addition, closed-environment vertical farms allow plant biochemists to fine-tune mineral nutrient dosing and ultraviolet light pulses during the final forty-eight hours prior to harvest, boosting antioxidant, vitamin C, and polyphenol concentrations in leafy vegetables by up to forty percent compared to field-grown produce. Municipal hospital networks, school meal programs, and urban grocery cooperatives increasingly partner directly with neighborhood vertical farms under multi-year supply agreements, insulating metropolitan residents from global supply-chain disruptions, extreme weather harvest failures, and volatile commodity price spikes. In conclusion, vertical urban farming represents a vital pillar of twenty-first-century metropolitan food security: transforming cities from passive, vulnerable food sinks into resilient, pesticide-free, self-sufficient agricultural ecosystems. Thank you for your attention."

That is the end of Section 4. You now have one minute to check your answers.`;

