  task1Prompt: "The table below illustrates the energy output (in Terawatt-hours) generated from four renewable sources across five countries in 2023. Summarise the main trends and highlight notable anomalies.",
  task1Model: `The table compares renewable electricity generation, measured in Terawatt-hours (TWh), across four distinct clean energy sectors—hydroelectric, wind, solar, and biomass—in five selected nations during 2023.

Overall, hydroelectric power was the predominant renewable source in the majority of surveyed countries, while solar and wind energy demonstrated substantial output in technologically advanced economies. Conversely, biomass contributed the lowest overall volume of electricity across all five nations.

Hydroelectric generation was overwhelmingly led by Country A and Country C, generating 340 TWh and 285 TWh respectively, dwarfing the modest outputs of Country D (45 TWh) and Country E (32 TWh). In contrast, wind power yielded its highest output in Country B at 180 TWh, closely followed by Country A at 165 TWh, whereas Country E produced merely 18 TWh.

Regarding solar electricity, Country B was again the foremost generator with 120 TWh, substantially surpassing Country C (58 TWh) and Country D (34 TWh). Finally, biomass recorded the lowest figures throughout the table, with Country A achieving the peak output of 42 TWh, while Country E registered an anomalous low of just 6 TWh.`,
  modelEssay: `In contemporary educational policy, the reallocation of instructional timetables from compulsory physical education to core academic subjects has generated intense debate among pedagogues, physicians, and curriculum developers. While proponents argue that intensifying academic instruction enhances international competitive standing in STEM disciplines, I firmly contend that marginalizing physical education represents a profoundly negative development that jeopardizes adolescent health, cognitive vitality, and social development.

Undeniably, advocates of academic prioritization operate under the conviction that globalized knowledge economies demand unprecedented scholastic specialization. In highly competitive university admissions environments, proficiency in advanced mathematics, computer programming, and natural sciences serves as the primary metric for socioeconomic mobility. From this perspective, dedicated athletic hours are frequently perceived as an unproductive luxury that could be more effectively utilized for examination drills and laboratory coursework.

However, this narrow pedagogical view disregards the fundamental neurobiological link between physical exercise and cognitive performance. Aerobic exertion stimulates the synthesis of Brain-Derived Neurotrophic Factor (BDNF), a vital protein that catalyzes neurogenesis and enhances synaptic plasticity within the hippocampus. Rigorous clinical trials confirm that students deprived of cardiovascular exertion exhibit diminished concentration spans, elevated anxiety, and lower academic stamina. Paradoxically, eliminating gym classes impairs the very cognitive faculties required for academic excellence.

Furthermore, replacing physical education exacerbates the global epidemic of juvenile sedentary lifestyles. Chronic ailments such as juvenile type-2 diabetes, cardiovascular distress, and childhood obesity impose catastrophic burdens on public healthcare infrastructures. Physical education instills lifelong athletic habits, resilience, and teamwork skills that cannot be acquired from textbooks alone.

In conclusion, although the demands of modern academic curricula are formidable, excising physical activity undermines the physiological and neurological foundation upon which intellectual excellence depends. It is therefore an unequivocally detrimental educational policy that modern secondary institutions must resist.`,
